#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "000408ba-6fbd-51da-9002-82fdde167a1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Point Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuities Exist"}] :question/id #uuid "6e182a7d-e877-546b-9ee2-1dc2a37e0fbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-3579" :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a removable discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/159566da49ef22ea1aae7800355949ae3cf02dac6a6a02e0a8bc344db176a75d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3.$ \n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff61d37b-595f-5dce-b4d5-fe70b3cf1d54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Point Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "35f49da3-296d-5b2a-8901-1650f6238468" :question/math-academy-id "e-3580" :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a point discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/10ba0496a4dc42579bb95d371a65d808edc0cc5a5653ab3e23d4fe85adf90518.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "d5f60234-7254-5707-926a-8f7b7b15e92a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3581" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x),$ defined by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ 3-x, & x>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+3)=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3-x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3,$ while the value of the function is $f(0)=0.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff61d37b-595f-5dce-b4d5-fe70b3cf1d54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Point Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e032a4a5-0609-5f20-bed6-7913dde2af26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/b77c43e9e33a958c65d6b3478b50c2f43e56d4a514bd092b50a9856d353a78f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307080} {:answer/id #uuid "02859795-38ef-5fda-8de2-4bedcb02ceb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/e82c2ab5ec147cbf2f6a6370aa10caaf23effdd672a2496add4bd72d2e5a9ab9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307081} {:answer/id #uuid "d5dfd831-51dd-5ac7-93bf-26ec100dc28e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/c6c9826ddd0cfc64cfa288b365c20c2e8bd67398f8f049a3ff80db13ab979d3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307082} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5bbf9c-4a90-5cd4-bb16-dc91c6793378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/340443e20bc285d2e711b81608a60eb81ff33537ece23860b11871f5373dfde2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307083} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd7f6f2-bd12-5f09-9020-2c0fa3970135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/f1b1db6867e4629ba3155f5de1d59cdf36ed9190dbd7a18733bcbcf2f10402c5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d5bbf9c-4a90-5cd4-bb16-dc91c6793378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/340443e20bc285d2e711b81608a60eb81ff33537ece23860b11871f5373dfde2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307083} :answer-field/id #uuid "b281959e-3f6b-58ea-a79f-4478c4c7b384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "069bab20-c8c9-55e2-ad80-81ddc7c330e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104387" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/340443e20bc285d2e711b81608a60eb81ff33537ece23860b11871f5373dfde2.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1$ but they are *not* removable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "000408ba-6fbd-51da-9002-82fdde167a1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Point Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuities Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7546b801-cf8d-5593-bf2a-5850d9b0b105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307087} {:answer/id #uuid "5477384e-c542-5ac2-b4a4-a2ea440e69ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307088} {:answer/id #uuid "6243536a-3068-5adf-b9b1-df9d3894ca3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\pm 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307089} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9eddb6-84ba-5677-80e5-c048f19372d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307090} {:answer/id #uuid "5548a840-8f1e-5294-a9d0-0b9c14bff39d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5477384e-c542-5ac2-b4a4-a2ea440e69ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307088} :answer-field/id #uuid "53fee554-6da1-513b-a77a-e622c404beb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0198f318-cd17-5473-a78f-6e641cab268c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104407" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/039c88a5cb4278b5522ccb95cde8a92f48ae572deb3ad38845c2ceaaec1e90ac.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but it is not a point removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-2)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-2$ is not a point removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "000408ba-6fbd-51da-9002-82fdde167a1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Point Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuities Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7361aca-5afb-5143-a3bc-8dea88fa3a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307094} {:answer/id #uuid "515e4c47-e56a-5b01-bcf7-fa2403a9cd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307095} {:answer/id #uuid "604ae4f7-9372-505d-8442-f40455fceb5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307096} {:answer/id #uuid "01e7db63-96fd-5041-a1cc-d7c10a7a2614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307097} {:answer/id #uuid "0a16e730-6882-588f-bea6-dd7edbfa4267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "604ae4f7-9372-505d-8442-f40455fceb5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307096} :answer-field/id #uuid "99fc37e3-ebcc-5b27-ab70-c5470beaad28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9d00c8f-1806-5984-8025-b0f458ce1c2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104415" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/8050047cf4381ef77d5569ac23b2adaa6b8484f5be672fd9a52bf011beeb39e8.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=-2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is also a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the first condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "000408ba-6fbd-51da-9002-82fdde167a1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Point Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuities Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30ae9b71-98ba-590d-a1cf-c4eb17c826eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307101} {:answer/id #uuid "a18d8c87-2cee-5776-9eea-acdc617efb74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307102} {:answer/id #uuid "f01d7c76-903c-5419-98f7-dddc8172bb2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307103} {:answer/id #uuid "80b73b78-4fca-598d-823b-c9b8b4cd9573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307104} {:answer/id #uuid "8934eea5-143d-5d6d-a996-7a3ee55caabd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8934eea5-143d-5d6d-a996-7a3ee55caabd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307105} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe83238f-fc1a-5a5e-b094-66d4ff80d344" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12cf0cf4-20ea-574d-b3f6-b08a331df572" :question/math-academy-id "q-104425" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/d869c61426504b7b24c2feebd60f68bca6f7dc8a2b40036bf5821e0208e1e197.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)\\ne f(5)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the second condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=5$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0468e042-4d2f-5f50-88f3-daa1e2261fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307108} {:answer/id #uuid "95688d2d-06a5-5284-ab99-5c0346344b8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307109} {:answer/id #uuid "bc0f1e3f-fcd5-5ff6-b081-5cccc4a0ecb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307110} {:answer/id #uuid "846e0a59-e5c5-5c8a-be6b-2edeaeeb50c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307111} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c7c874-af2c-5ef6-be91-51c99748dd80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc0f1e3f-fcd5-5ff6-b081-5cccc4a0ecb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307110} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd41f9d3-e177-5f93-88a2-3ef6be301080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d16c9ba-34ea-5f9d-9f33-6f6c8299fea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104572" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+11}, & x<5 \\\\ 12, & x=5 \\\\ {e}^{5/x}, & x>5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f(5)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)$\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(5)=12$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}\\sqrt{x+11}=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}{e}^{5/x}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de29c919-b9b2-5bfd-8e28-8866520f43e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307115} {:answer/id #uuid "3251f918-66c1-5662-a1b6-f41a93bab800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307116} {:answer/id #uuid "2597d338-1935-51bc-84a3-21998c272e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307117} {:answer/id #uuid "82b4ff1d-0067-5205-b166-d2e7e17d665e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307118} {:answer/id #uuid "6565f2d4-7491-56db-b42e-234169d48802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6565f2d4-7491-56db-b42e-234169d48802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307119} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f518b1-8550-51a9-b688-877ceaaec56d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9a3f2db-4157-50dc-996e-676dd4243a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104575" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-34, & x<-3 \\\\ -25, & x=-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2, & x>-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(-3)=-25$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}({x}^{2}-34)=(-3)^{2}-34=-25, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}({x}^{3}+2)=(-3)^{3}+2=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25,$ and the value of the function is $g(-3)=-25.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=-3,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff61d37b-595f-5dce-b4d5-fe70b3cf1d54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Point Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e299d61e-d811-575f-8652-9c2781d8496e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307122} {:answer/id #uuid "4e308afc-b327-5302-9136-42a1190555f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307123} {:answer/id #uuid "87435c62-1d55-50c2-98cd-341e39a0afcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307124} {:answer/id #uuid "64db1116-b182-5719-b04e-614c938eb7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307125} {:answer/id #uuid "5dae35eb-5ec0-53d0-af04-382decd66161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dae35eb-5ec0-53d0-af04-382decd66161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307126} :answer-field/id #uuid "da18e595-bfb7-5db8-b733-c988c6b371bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f6ba6a9-669c-53ec-9eb7-450cfe9629e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-47516" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/4e796ff4d915d8c8b829574ade4eab05c7740de1f376d00900ec0d14b08f97d9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$\n- There are removable discontinuities at both $x=-1$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nOn the other hand, the function has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)\\ne f(1)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, II is true while I and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff61d37b-595f-5dce-b4d5-fe70b3cf1d54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Point Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3634d29f-864c-5612-a3a0-2d1adfd25709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307129} {:answer/id #uuid "75e55be7-ecaf-5ce3-9ace-15055cf1d531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307130} {:answer/id #uuid "12828aa8-0496-5f7b-b71e-512ec654adf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307131} {:answer/id #uuid "6c868039-c6bf-5e41-a903-d151527d60bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307132} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8fba5c-4423-5a82-823f-7e602b0e9309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3634d29f-864c-5612-a3a0-2d1adfd25709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307129} :answer-field/id #uuid "b083d42b-d207-593c-9314-275453cff8f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66cc33ab-f2ce-599f-8f13-d60a316244d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47559" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/613eaf413822013885a22df61a4bf32f7cd763bf11db71ab7b8769e19c0369b0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$\n- It is continuous at $x=3.$\n- It has removable discontinuities at both $x=0$ and $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(0)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)\\ne f(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, I and III are true while II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff61d37b-595f-5dce-b4d5-fe70b3cf1d54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Point Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a35281b-4721-56cd-8095-dbe034073f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/937d91014870e27a93c247e87b7008f5c63807df764503fc7a9ebccfa8044d65.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307136} {:answer/id #uuid "2cba0e29-d85c-5b4f-922d-2c8134e3bdff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/5c22017c6a89ddb6a4ff481de6b210e272075ca2e7d492785d4be4950982cce0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307137} {:answer/id #uuid "8d59779b-2d58-5f31-be35-d65255227a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/fbc65d4168ce2f6747e75a606b3ccc175f9a68d98f718e473255f085d929c334.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307138} {:answer/id #uuid "8b30df25-76d2-59d7-af44-cd8741448dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/ebef782bf2d2df29c0b016e5f2e3ae42eab200f3ae502d1eea7fe522ff0f1b5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307139} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b2748c-4292-5956-a2d4-979418b9dbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/bbe9ecff2a793d2cadb42c6dedfab841869fae3215f6da0d8b6da7b9fbfa4ec1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d59779b-2d58-5f31-be35-d65255227a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/fbc65d4168ce2f6747e75a606b3ccc175f9a68d98f718e473255f085d929c334.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307138} :answer-field/id #uuid "99f5a4c9-0afd-5eec-a774-22b631ae61ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8a1f245-c03d-5454-853a-837712e2f33c" :question/math-academy-id "q-48111" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has a removable discontinuity at $x=2?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $p(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $p(2)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}p(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}p(x)\\ne p(2)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/fbc65d4168ce2f6747e75a606b3ccc175f9a68d98f718e473255f085d929c334.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=2,$ while $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=2$ but they are *not* removable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff61d37b-595f-5dce-b4d5-fe70b3cf1d54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Point Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b67527a-f4e3-5391-a905-44fcbfa0c672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128193} {:answer/id #uuid "a876e01a-f3b7-5747-aca5-51b1fca57206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128194} {:answer/id #uuid "db976f25-01f6-5b75-ae8c-da560e14b229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128195} {:answer/id #uuid "16df2d72-856b-5be2-8cd2-6a2abe45ba08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128196} {:answer/id #uuid "56da6571-83cb-5c12-b88e-df10c545f97f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b67527a-f4e3-5391-a905-44fcbfa0c672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128193} :answer-field/id #uuid "55be8106-456e-5f7d-9b4e-e58db69282d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89af0137-085e-52c8-8599-1a8b5ce6239a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48115" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has a removable discontinuity at $x=3?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $q(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $q(3)=0\\,$  exists  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}q(x)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}q(x)\\ne q(3)$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/d7fc2426919c72ff934d19e98eb7465b8a42852407809d242213da9c26a4ed4a.png)\n\n   \n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=3,$ while $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=3$ but they are *not* removable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984d002d-b3d9-5fff-85b5-920571715f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307143} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c0e10c-0f05-5103-b9fc-adcc899df027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307144} {:answer/id #uuid "a031c657-25d5-51ae-a85c-2e621b1d29e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307145} {:answer/id #uuid "30cfde9a-83c4-5a38-bea3-ac4bf8d7ca24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307146} {:answer/id #uuid "da74d5a3-27c1-5574-aea9-a73dce167a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a031c657-25d5-51ae-a85c-2e621b1d29e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307145} :answer-field/id #uuid "e49579b5-6c9a-537b-afe7-9a57de71cbf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a51cbbd3-b785-5c40-be75-e613ee48e2aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-48125" :question/problem "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}+3 & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x=0 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>0\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2(x+1), & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x>0\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+12x+1, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $f(0)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{0}+3=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3(0)+4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=4.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $g(0)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & =2(0+1)=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & ={0}^{2}+2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x)\\ne g(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $h(0)=2(0)^{2}+1=1$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x) & =2(0)^{2}+1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x) & =(0)^{5}+12(0)+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x)=h(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $g(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "000408ba-6fbd-51da-9002-82fdde167a1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Point Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuities Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fba041a-00f4-56c2-b579-8ed9efc55eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307150} {:answer/id #uuid "2500e4a4-aaad-5b59-ab49-e3398e8d6c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307151} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1dd774-f727-53a8-82a9-f85ec6f386f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307152} {:answer/id #uuid "1238ed40-c4bf-5e3f-8056-29ddaeb4f20b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307153} {:answer/id #uuid "87ac35e5-2b80-5f8a-9137-9fd3de35128f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1238ed40-c4bf-5e3f-8056-29ddaeb4f20b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307153} :answer-field/id #uuid "a95f6f1a-b250-5e5f-a49b-9a562bd57875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "660180ee-565e-5382-abb3-a2ce13e9f578" :question/math-academy-id "q-48128" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/e3fa6d490da81fca9b24f9de41b01daaa957f574375cc8523aec448a0ef73015.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-2)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ but it is not a point discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(2)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=2$ is not a point discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-2$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "000408ba-6fbd-51da-9002-82fdde167a1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Point Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuities Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d870378f-95df-54d3-a64c-6e38f02a29ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307157} {:answer/id #uuid "b2358219-bf2c-5892-89f4-753e3439b201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307158} {:answer/id #uuid "62cfe893-faeb-5f5b-8512-a69e4e3743d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307159} {:answer/id #uuid "24b373c4-4db7-5b53-9c33-d3b529edd178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307160} {:answer/id #uuid "5733a9d1-18ad-519e-9ec9-9a97247561af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\pm 3$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62cfe893-faeb-5f5b-8512-a69e4e3743d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307159} :answer-field/id #uuid "24f32f4f-d2ca-5fdc-8291-f4fdb87542c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a85b78fd-d65c-5ded-9498-c7b6781a7e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48161" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/b92c2d0e713799d32f1be12b6cba56231af27ccc7a013e8726ab3b09289e69d8.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a point discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but it is not a point discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-3)$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the first condition failed, and therefore $x=-3$ is not a point discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13eb3e3b-398e-5705-8b37-faf0d0221d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307164} {:answer/id #uuid "ffbf2054-544b-55d2-825d-51d4b8e04592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307165} {:answer/id #uuid "0b99ee23-faf9-553a-8c85-62f5df9316bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307166} {:answer/id #uuid "90eb723c-4b84-571c-bd2e-6c5145c1cd52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307167} {:answer/id #uuid "14c35b4e-8ac4-5203-aa7e-37b1b63cc176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13eb3e3b-398e-5705-8b37-faf0d0221d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307164} :answer-field/id #uuid "528c1cd4-5920-56e4-a2e9-82178e232930" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc1ce46c-5f51-5585-8b29-6e546ebe7e4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71179" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 13, & x=4,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=g(4)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(4)=13$ exists$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}3x=12, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}3x=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12,$ while the value of the function is $g(4)=13.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)\\ne g(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=4,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86978994-32c7-540e-9c72-afdae08db11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128433} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d5ee4d-89d9-50cc-80f6-86fa63e36b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128434} {:answer/id #uuid "f80e4162-7bf6-506d-a86d-3a92205369a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128435} {:answer/id #uuid "52080794-e2d5-596e-8b69-ce656836e0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128436} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4d2e19-f4e5-5079-a4c2-8e82c75ec276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f80e4162-7bf6-506d-a86d-3a92205369a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128435} :answer-field/id #uuid "54fef6ec-5613-5739-be97-f0d1c6191169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de45d0f1-1f5a-51b7-a854-73bfcba5bbdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71181" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x-5, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(1)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(5x-5)=5(1)-5=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={1}^{3}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0,$ and the value of the function is $g(1)=0.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=0,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}]}
